# netsurf: precise sibling-combinator risk for class changes (replaces patch 174 blanket decline)
# Generated 2026-10-01
#
# Patch 174 declined every block-level class change when any author
# stylesheet contained a '+' or '~' in a selector. That is sound but far
# too broad: most such rules (e.g. `.menu + .content`) do not involve the
# element whose class changed, forcing a full rebuild for unrelated CSS.
#
# This patch replaces the blanket scan with a precise per-selector check:
# for every sibling combinator in every author stylesheet, the compound
# immediately to its left is parsed once and cached per sheet. When a
# block's class changes from OLD to NEW, each left compound is matched
# against the element twice -- once with the OLD class set, once with the
# NEW -- using explicit values from the mutation record, never live DOM.
# The change is safe for the local path iff no left compound's match state
# differs (XOR): a compound matching under both, or under neither, means no
# following sibling's cascade can have changed through that combinator.
#
# Measurements: the dedicated CSS-sibling-class.html gate (6 cases) passes.
# Existing gates preserved: JS-class-stall, JS-block-class-stall,
# JS-block-class-sibling, and the text/block local-rebuild gates remain
# green (verified in the simulator gate run).
#
# Conservative decline cases (full rebuild, always correct):
# - '+'/'~' inside :has(), :is(), :not(), :where() (e.g. `.t:has(+ .u)`
#   restyles a PRECEDING sibling, unreachable by the one-block rebuild).
# - A pseudo-class on a left compound (e.g. `.a:hover + .b`): dynamic state
#   cannot be evaluated exactly here.
# - Any selector prelude that fails to parse, or a combinator where a
#   compound was expected.
# - The old class value is unknown (NULL from the mutation record).
# - Arithmetic '+' in :nth-child(2n+1) is not a combinator and is ignored.
# - The patch-174 (source pointer, size) cache is replaced with a
#   stylesheet-handle keyed cache validated by FNV-1a hash, closing the
#   allocator-reuse aliasing hole.
#
# Test: testpages/pages/CSS-sibling-class.html (6 cases: unrelated sibling
# CSS stays local; genuine + and ~ decline correctly; :has(+ ...) declines;
# multiple combinators; nth-child arithmetic not mistaken).
